<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Pocock</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1706">1706</a>–<a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir George Pocock was a British admiral. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> he fought three indecisive battles against the French fleet off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1758–1759, and in 1762 he commanded the fleet that, with the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-keppel-3rd-earl-of-albemarle">Earl of Albemarle</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He retired soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-pocock.jpg" alt="George Pocock" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Thomas Hudson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_George_Pocock_by_Thomas_Hudson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George Pocock was born on 6 March 1706 at Thames Ditton in Surrey, the son of Thomas Pocock, a chaplain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>; his family had long lived at Chieveley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>. He entered the navy in 1718 aboard HMS Superb under the patronage of his maternal uncle, Captain Streynsham Master. He became a lieutenant in April 1725, a commander in 1733 and a post-captain in 1738, when he was given the 20-gun HMS Aldborough. After service in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> he joined Rear-Admiral Charles Watson in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> in 1754 as captain of the 58-gun HMS Cumberland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Watson's squadron co-operated with Clive in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and Pocock rose to rear-admiral in 1755 and vice-admiral in 1756. On Watson's death in 1757 he took command in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>. His task was to stop French reinforcements reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>. The squadron of Comte D'Aché was weaker ship for ship, as it included armed Indiamen, but it was more numerous, and the fighting instructions of the time obliged Pocock to engage in a line and keep his formation.</p><p>The first battle, on 29 April 1758, did not stop the French reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>; after a heavier action on 3 August D'Aché withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>. Pocock relieved the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> the next spring, and a hard-fought battle on 10 September 1759 was again indecisive. The French government, nearly bankrupt, could not supply D'Aché, who returned to the islands, leaving the British in possession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coasts</a>. Pocock went home in 1760 and in 1761 was made a Knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> and admiral.</p><p>In 1762 he commanded the naval forces of the expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. The siege lasted from 7 June to 13 August and the climate made it deadly; the final victory owed much to the help Pocock gave the troops, and his share of the prize money was £122,697.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pocock is said to have resigned because Sir Charles Saunders was chosen instead of him for the Admiralty Board; it is certain that he resigned his commission in 1766. In 1763 he married Sophia Dent, widow of Commodore Digby Dent; their son George was created a baronet. Pocock died on 3 April 1792. His memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, a statue of Britannia holding a thunderbolt by John Bacon, was erected in 1796.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Pocock” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Spain entered the Seven Years' War, a British expedition under George Keppel, Earl of Albemarle, and Admiral Sir <b>George Pocock</b> landed near Havana in June 1762.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-havana">British occupation of Havana</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Curzon Street, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.42,-1&amp;year=1761" aria-label="Berkshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4527 383 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George Pocock"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4977 653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4977 653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.42,-1&amp;year=1761">What was here in 1761? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
